# ComposeUX ↔ Home Assistant Bridge [SPEC:CXC-HA-BRIDGE]

> Home Assistant is the HUB — the central engine that owns integrations, the very shape ComposeIO inverts.
> This spec doesn't fight it; it **demotes** it. The HA-Bridge is a ComposeBus peer that puts a *running* HA
> instance ON the bus — its entities become semantic namespaces, a ComposeBus command becomes an HA service
> call — so HA sits alongside the $0.10 MCUs and the Intelligences as one node among many, kept for the one
> thing it's unmatched at (thousands of open-source integrations) but no longer the center. There are exactly
> two clean paths to the HA/ESPHome ecosystem, and **neither runs a plugin inside ComposeIO**: MINE the source
> to author native drivers ([CXC-HALD-MINE], design-time), or BRIDGE a running instance (this spec, runtime).

---

## 1. Intent

### [CXC-HAB-INTENT] The Hub Becomes a Peer

- definition: a running HA instance exposes its state + services over MQTT (its `statestream`) and a WebSocket API. The bridge is a ComposeBus peer that MIRRORS that surface onto the bus, bidirectionally, so any bus participant — a widget, a leaf rule, an Intelligence, another device — reads and commands HA entities as ordinary semantic namespaces, ignorant that HA is behind them. HA runs its own integrations; ComposeIO runs none.
- bridge, not runner: HA integrations are coupled to HA core and are NOT runnable outside it, so the only ways to reach them are to bridge the running instance (here) or mine their source ([CXC-HALD-MINE]). This spec explicitly does NOT run or emulate HA plugins — it talks to HA-as-a-whole, which is already running them.
- the inversion: putting HA on the bus is the hub-model reflex turned inside out — the hub keeps doing what it is good at, but as a SUBORDINATE peer; the control plane is the bus, not HA. For a maker who has HA but doesn't want to live in it, this is the right relationship: harvest its value, don't sit inside it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HA-BRIDGE]

---

## 2. The Bridge

### [CXC-HAB-INGRESS] HA Entities → ComposeBus Namespaces

- definition: the bridge subscribes HA's state stream (MQTT `statestream` topics `homeassistant/<domain>/<entity>/state`, or the WebSocket `state_changed` events) and publishes each MAPPED entity to a ComposeBus semantic namespace by PHYSICAL QUANTITY ([CXC-HALD-NAMESPACE]) — `sensor.living_room_temp` → `env/temperature`, `binary_sensor.front_door` → `contact/door`, `light.desk` → `light/on` + `light/brightness`. The HA domain + attributes inform the mapping; the bus sees the QUANTITY, not the HA entity id.
- attributes + availability: an entity's attributes fan into sub-namespaces (a light's brightness/color, a climate entity's setpoint/current); HA `unavailable`/`unknown` maps to the health/diagnostic convention ([CXC-HALD-NAMESPACE] `.../health`).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HA-BRIDGE]

### [CXC-HAB-EGRESS] ComposeBus Commands → HA Service Calls

- definition: a write to a mapped COMMAND namespace becomes an HA SERVICE CALL — `light/on = true` → `light.turn_on{entity_id}`; `light/brightness = 0.5` → `light.turn_on{brightness_pct:50}`; `scene/mood = "storm"` → `scene.turn_on`; `cover/position = 0.3` → `cover.set_cover_position`. Delivered via HA's MQTT command topics or the WebSocket `call_service`. So a ComposeBus director/rule/Intelligence actuates HA-managed devices without knowing HA is behind them — the same `light/on` a native WLED adaptor or an MCU relay consumes.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HA-BRIDGE]

### [CXC-HAB-MAPPING] The Declarative, Default-Deny Entity ↔ Namespace Map

- definition: a declarative map — `{ ha_entity ↔ namespace(s), direction, transform? }` — that is **default-DENY** (only listed entities cross the bridge; a whole HA install does not flood the bus). It reuses the by-physical-quantity vocabulary, so a screen gauge or a leaf rule binds `env/temperature` indifferent to whether a NATIVE sensor or an HA entity is behind it — the define-once payoff, extended across the HA boundary. Auto-SUGGEST from HA's entity registry (domain → default namespace) with explicit overrides; a `transform` handles unit/scale/enum coercion.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HA-BRIDGE]

---

## 3. The Peer

### [CXC-HAB-ADAPTOR] An Adaptor-Family App (Graceful if Absent)

- definition: the bridge is an `adaptor`-family peer ([CXC-HALD-KINDS] / [CXC-HALD-BUILT]) — a host ComposeApp, sibling to the built WLED adaptor and the Intelligence MCP — configured `interface: { adaptor: { ha: { url, token | mqtt } } }`. Its internal↔ComposeBus membrane ([CXC-HALD-BRIDGE]) EXPORTS the mapped HA namespaces up; IMPORT drives HA. If HA is unreachable it degrades gracefully: the bridged namespaces go `health=down`, and the rest of the bus is UNAFFECTED (encapsulation).
- optional, never required: ComposeBus does not depend on HA; the bridge exists for a deployment that HAS HA and wants it as a peer. A maker with no HA simply never instantiates it — and can still MINE HA/ESPHome source for native drivers with no HA runtime at all.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HA-BRIDGE]

### [CXC-HAB-AUTHORITY] Authority Across the Bridge

- definition: EGRESS (ComposeBus → HA service call) that actuates a real load is AUTHORITY-GATED ([CXC-HALD-BRIDGE] authority), so a random bus peer cannot command HA-managed devices; INGRESS is scoped by the default-deny map (not every HA entity is exported). This is bus hygiene per the HAL membrane model — the same discipline as `gm/cmd/*` and a `control`-class actuator.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-HA-BRIDGE]

---

## 4. Verification (what a build must satisfy)

| id | claim | test |
|---|---|---|
| HAB-C1 | HA entity → namespace | a mapped `sensor.*` publishes `env/temperature`; a bus `gauge` reads it indifferent to source |
| HAB-C2 | command → service call | `light/on=true` on the bus results in `light.turn_on` on a mock HA |
| HAB-C3 | default-deny + by-quantity | only mapped entities bridge; each lands on a by-physical-quantity namespace, not an HA entity id |
| HAB-C4 | graceful absence | HA unreachable → bridged namespaces go `health=down`; the rest of the bus is unaffected |
| HAB-C5 | no plugin hosting | the bridge runs NO HA integration code — only HA's MQTT/WebSocket surface is touched |
| HAB-C6 | authority | an egress that actuates a load is authority-gated; a non-authorized peer's command is dropped |
| HAB-C7 | define-once across the boundary | a rule bound to `env/temperature` fires identically whether a native DS18B20 or a bridged HA sensor is the source |

---

